"At the beginning of this surah, it is recounted that the jinn listened to the Quran and believed. The word 'nafar' denotes between three and ten individuals, yet it can also be used for a greater number. Since it is also used for the jinn, it is not exclusive to human beings. The verb 'we listened' mentioned in the verse is passive and is read with an upper vowel mark (fathah) in the recitations. The sentence 'They said: Indeed, we' is read with a kasrah, because it indicates reported speech.\n\nThe word 'hidayah' means the truth and the upright path. The connection between the verses varies according to the differences in recitations. While in some recitations it is connected to 'we heard', in the Hafs recitation it is read with a fathah, yielding the meaning 'we believed in it'. In the expression 'Exalted is the majesty of our Lord', 'jadd' means greatness, glory, and sublimity. Here, Allah's sublime attribute is intended. The expression 'our foolish one' describes the rebellious jinn or Iblis; 'shatat', on the other hand, means exceeding the boundary and swerving from the truth.\n\nCertain individuals among humankind used to take refuge in certain individuals among the jinn. This seeking of refuge took place through practices such as talismans and incantations. The word 'rijal' (men) is used here for males of both humankind and jinn. According to the majority of scholars, this demonstrates that the jinn also consist of males and females. The word 'rahaq' conveys meanings such as covering, frivolousness, and transgression; humans seeking refuge in the jinn increased the jinn's arrogance and transgression.\n\nThe jinn had assumed that Allah would never send a messenger. However, when they probed the heaven, they found it filled with formidable guards and flaming fires. 'Shihab' means a flame of fire. These flames prevented the jinn from eavesdropping on tidings of the unseen. In reality, this symbolizes the light of the Quran and prophethood obliterating falsehood. Through this occurrence, the jinn realized that Allah may send a messenger to whomever He wills.\n\nThe jinn said: 'We do not know whether evil is intended for those on earth, or whether good is willed for them.' They admitted that there were righteous and base individuals among them, and that they were divided into distinct factions. They recognized that they could never frustrate Allah nor flee from Him. They listened to the Quran and believed; whoever believes will neither be wronged of their due nor fall into humiliation. Those who submit as Muslims are worthy of goodness, while the deviators are deserving of Hell.\n\nAllah declares: 'If humans and jinn had remained steadfast on the upright path, We would have provided them with abundant sustenance. This is a trial. Whoever turns away from the remembrance of their Lord, He will thrust into an arduous punishment.' 'The places of worship (masajid) belong to Allah; so do not supplicate anyone alongside Him.' Masajid signifies sanctuaries. Some scholars have interpreted this as the entire earth or the places of prostration. Allah commands that worship be devoted exclusively to Him.\n\nWhen the servant of Allah (the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him) stood up supplicating to Him, the jinn or the polytheists crowded around him so densely that they were almost pressing into one another. The Prophet was instructed: 'Say: I pray only to my Lord and associate no partner with Him. I possess no power to cause you harm or benefit; I only convey the messages and embassies of Allah. Whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger, the fire of Hell belongs to them. I do not know whether that which is promised is near or far; only Allah knows the unseen. He discloses His unseen to no one except His chosen messengers.'\n\nAllah Almighty commands His Prophet: Say, no one can protect me from Allah. That is to say, there is no power that can shield against the punishment of Allah. If I were to associate partners with Allah or supplicate to other than Him, neither human, nor jinn, nor angel could save me. For this reason, I supplicate only to Allah, and there is no sanctuary besides Him. Nor should you fear me or anyone else; fear only your Lord and take refuge in Him. This verse might lead one to presume that it is impermissible to place any hope in or ask anything of the Prophet; however, if this judgment is passed without taking the exception into account, it would be mistaken. Indeed, although the Wahhabis considered taking the Prophet as a means of mediation (wasilah) to be associating partners with Allah, this is not correct.\n\nIn verse 23, an exception is introduced: \"Except for notification from Allah and His messages.\" This is an exception to the preceding statement, \"I possess no power to cause you harm or guide you of my own accord.\" That is to say, the Prophet can do nothing of his own accord; rather, he is charged with conveying Allah's commandments. This is precisely what is expected of the Prophet. At the same time, he serves as a means for the supplication of the servants, for Allah has commanded, \"Pray for them, so that it may bring peace to their hearts.\" Just as believers are not forbidden from requesting supplication from one another, asking for supplication from the Prophet is not forbidden either. This exception has conferred great honor and authority upon the Prophet.\n\nFor those who disobey Allah and His Messenger, there is the fire of Hell, wherein they will abide eternally. This warning encompasses those who disbelieve by associating partners with Allah. In verse 24, it is stated that when they behold the punishment promised to them, they will come to know who is weaker in supporters and fewer in number. On that day, the rebellious among humans and jinn, who deemed themselves mighty and numerous, will see how feeble they truly are. In verse 25, when asked when that punishment will come, the Prophet answers: \"Without a notification from my Lord, I cannot know. Whether that which is promised to you is near or far is not known; the knowledge thereof is solely with Allah.\"\n\nAllah knows the entirety of the unseen (ghayb). The unseen denotes that which is not present to sensory perception or acquired knowledge. The absolute unseen cannot be known by any created being; however, Allah discloses aspects of His unseen to those messengers whom He chooses. This disclosure pertains to the proofs of messendership, legal rulings, and sanctions. The messenger knows only what has been disclosed to him; he does not know the absolute unseen. Matters such as the timing of the Hour are exclusively within the knowledge of Allah. When Allah reveals His unseen, He posts guardian angels before and behind His messenger to protect him, thereby preventing the jinn and devils from interfering with the revelation. They are driven away with flaming meteors (shihab); this is a miracle exclusive to the prophethood of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him).\n\nThe reason Allah posts these guardian watchers is so that the messenger may know with certainty (yaqin) that the revelation reaching him is truly from Allah. Thus, the messenger realizes that he has conveyed the word of his Lord precisely as it is, and that Allah encompasses all things in His knowledge. Following the elucidations concerning \"what you see and what you do not see\" sworn to in Surah al-Haqqah, and after describing the Prophet's sublime character and the manner in which revelation descends, this surah will continue with Surah al-Muzzammil."